# Craft — comment-discipline

## The governing rule

> "Comments should describe things that are not obvious from the code." — [Ousterhout, CS 190](https://web.stanford.edu/~ouster/cgi-bin/cs190-winter18/lecture.php?topic=comments)

Both of his named failures are in scope, and they point opposite ways: *"Mistake #1: comments duplicate code"* and *"Mistake #2: non-obvious info is not described"*. Silence where the reason lives is the same defect as noise where the code already speaks.

## What earns one

- **The why.** Comments *"explain why some code exists, and should not be explaining what some code is doing"* — with the exception shipped alongside the rule: *"regular expressions and complex algorithms often benefit greatly from comments that explain what they're doing"* ([Google eng-practices](https://google.github.io/eng-practices/review/reviewer/looking-for.html)).
- **The three-clause gate** ([Google pyguide §3.8](https://google.github.io/styleguide/pyguide.html)): *"A docstring is mandatory for every function that has one or more of the following properties: being part of the public API · nontrivial size · non-obvious logic."* Its contrapositive is the clause that suppresses — private **and** small **and** obvious ⇒ ship it bare.
- **Precision a reader cannot infer**: units, ownership, nullability, the invariant a field holds, what a return value promises. *"Lower level comments provide precision (especially for variables, arguments, return values)."*
- **Intuition the code cannot hold**: *"Higher-level comments capture intuition"* — what a block accomplishes whole, the constraint that shaped it.
- **A `TODO` carrying an owner or a tracker**: `// TODO(username): Describe what needs to be done.` (Google C++) · `# TODO: crbug.com/192795 - Investigate cpufreq optimizations.` (Google Python).
- **The test for leaving one out**, Ousterhout: *"Skip comments only if you're sure it will be obvious to readers."* And pyguide's, for the ones you keep: *"never describe the code. Assume the person reading the code knows Python (though not what you're trying to do) better than you do."*

## Where it sits

- **At a different level of detail than the line it accompanies** — above it (purpose, intuition) or below it (precision, contract). At the same level it is the line restated, and the signature already carried it: *"Do not add boilerplate kernel-doc which simply reiterates what's obvious from the signature of the function"* ([Linux kernel, ch. 8](https://www.kernel.org/doc/html/latest/process/coding-style.html)).
- **Interface documentation carries what a caller must know; the algorithm belongs inside the body.** Ousterhout: *"Important to separate these: do not describe the implementation in the interface documentation!"* Go states it normatively: *"Doc comments should not explain internal details such as the algorithm used in the current implementation. Those are best left to comments inside the function body"* ([go.dev/doc/comment](https://go.dev/doc/comment)).

## Refactor first

> "Comments aren't a bad smell; indeed they are a sweet smell." […] "comments are often used as a deodorant." — [Fowler, *Refactoring*](https://www.informit.com/articles/article.aspx?p=2952392&seqNum=24)

*"When you feel the need to write a comment, first try to refactor the code so that any comment becomes superfluous."* In order: **Extract Function** → **Change Function Declaration** → **Introduce Assertion**. What survives that escalation has earned its line.

## Carve-outs — language convention outranks sparseness

- **Go**: *"Every exported (capitalized) name should have a doc comment"* ([go.dev/doc/comment](https://go.dev/doc/comment)). Write it on every exported name, even when it echoes the name.
- **Rust**: every public item carries an example — C-EXAMPLE, [API guidelines](https://rust-lang.github.io/api-guidelines/documentation.html). Praxis follows a language's own rules rather than making a crate fail them.
- **The user's instruction wins.** A teaching repo or a tutorial wants heavy commentary; give it heavy commentary. `PRAXIS_COMMENTS=off` is the mechanical form of that instruction.

## Named anti-patterns

`hooks/pre-tool-use` denies these four on added lines. The fix in every case is to delete the line.

- **Commented-out code** — git holds the history.
- **Banner and divider comments** — `// =====`, `/* ---- */`.
- **Chain-of-thought leakage** — `// Step 1:`, `// Now let's`, `// As requested`, and anything else referring to the conversation instead of to the code.
- **A `TODO` with no owner and no tracker.**
